# Open Source Documentation Standards

## Core Principles
- Write for a developer audience: clear, concise, and scannable.
- Avoid jargon; explain non-standard architecture choices immediately.
- Keep sentences short and use active voice.

## Markdown & Structure
- Every major feature or module needs a clear heading hierarchy (`#`, `##`, `###`).
- Include a quick copy-pasteable code snippet for installation and basic usage.
- Use callouts (`> [!NOTE]` or `> [!WARNING]`) for important caveats or breaking changes.
- Add markdown badges or other special objects if you think can help visualization.

## Code Comments & Docstrings
- Require JSDoc for JavaScript/TypeScript functions and Google-style docstrings for Python.
- Explain *why* a complex logic choice was made, not just *what* the code does.
- Keep code examples inside documentation up to date with the latest API changes.
- Keep code comments shorts, max 3 lines.
